Ù4Ye shall not afflict any widow, or fatherless child.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ\If thou afflict them in any wise, and they cry at all unto me, I will surely hear their cry;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ}and my wrath shall wax hot, and I will kill you with the sword; and your wives shall be widows, and your children fatherless.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŠIf thou lend money to any of my people that is poor by thee, thou shalt not be to him as an usurer, neither shalt thou lay upon him usury.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙqIf thou at all take thy neighbour's raiment to pledge, thou shalt deliver it unto him by that the sun goeth down:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ®for that is his covering only, it is his raiment for his skin: wherein shall he sleep? and it shall come to pass, when he crieth unto me, that I will hear; for I am gracious.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses‘„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙBThou shalt not revile the gods, nor curse the ruler of thy people.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‚Thou shalt not delay to offer the first of thy ripe fruits, and of thy liquors: the firstborn of thy sons shalt thou give unto me.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‰Likewise shalt thou do with thine oxen, and with thy sheep: seven days it shall be with his dam; on the eighth day thou shalt give it me.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses‘„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙƒAnd ye shall be holy men unto me: neither shall ye eat any flesh that is torn of beasts in the field; ye shall cast it to the dogs.¦layout ªlast_verse